<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/monsoons-and-tropical-climates">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A monsoon is a <strong>giant seasonal wind that reverses with the sun</strong> — pulling ocean
    moisture onto land in summer, draining it back in winter. The Asian monsoon is the planet's
    largest weather system, delivering fresh water to roughly half of humanity and deciding
    harvests, floods and economies on a schedule farmers have planned around for millennia.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the seasonal machine and its stakes" widget="equationStep" config={monsoonCfg} />

  <h2>Tropical climates beyond the monsoon</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The ITCZ — the equatorial rain belt:</strong> where trade winds converge, air rises and rain falls daily. It migrates with the sun; its seasonal passage sets wet and dry seasons across the tropics. Its failure = drought (the Sahel's tragedies are ITCZ variations).</li>
    <li><strong>Tropical cyclones:</strong> heat engines over warm oceans (26.5 °C+); categories measure wind, but storm surge kills most. Bangladesh's shelter systems cut cyclone deaths from ~300,000 (1970) to double digits in comparable storms.</li>
    <li><strong>Tropical soils are fragile:</strong> heavy rain leaches nutrients; fertility often sits in the vegetation, not the soil — clearing rainforest yields 2–3 good harvests before exhaustion. The soils lesson in earth science expands the story.</li>
    <li><strong>Climate change sharpens extremes:</strong> warmer air holds ~7% more water per °C — monsoon rains arrive more intensely, with longer dry spells between. The bounty and the hazard are both growing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> IMD monsoon reports; Gadgil, "The Indian monsoon and its variability"; IPCC AR6 (extreme precipitation).</p>
</TopicPage>
